  "schemaVersion": 2,
  "matching": "Judge the note as the person wrote it, before phone numbers are removed: the replacement token adds English-looking words that would hide a non-Latin note. Fold the note as the evidence pre-screen does (NFKD, drop combining marks, lowercase) and split on any code point that is not a letter or digit; drop words made only of digits. A word is non-Latin when it holds a letter outside a to z after folding. Count E, the words in `english`, and F, the words in `foreign` plus the non-Latin words. The note does NOT look English, and is refused for the cloud read, when more than half of its words are non-Latin, or when F is at least `minForeign` and F is more than `foreignOverEnglish` times E. Every other note, including one with no words, looks English. Version 2 splits the foreign words by language and adds noteLanguage: S counts the words in `spanish`, O the words in `portuguese` or `vietnamese` plus the non-Latin words, and `sharedForeign` words count toward F but toward neither S nor O. F is S plus O plus the shared words, so the refuse condition above is unchanged. A refused note is `es` when S is more than O, and `other` otherwise; a note that is not refused is `en`.",
  "source": "OURS: language screen before a cloud read. English notes quoting Spanish names or phrases pass; whole notes in Spanish, Portuguese, Vietnamese or Korean are refused. Known miss: a very short foreign note with few function words (for example \"Ella no esta aqui\") passes. Tuned on shared/test-vectors/language.json and on 325 English sentences from this repository's docs (0 refused). Version 2 draft: the v1 foreign words split by language, less four that are everyday English (as, do, hay, sin), Spanish words a family or a searcher types added, and cloudLanguages, which stays [\"en\"] until the Spanish phone-number and evidence checks ship on every platform.",
